1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,379
August again. The
Dembi Dollo rain hasn't

2
00:00:02,379 --> 00:00:04,680
changed—but your voice,
the one that warmed this

3
00:00:04,680 --> 00:00:05,680
house, is gone.

4
00:00:08,250 --> 00:00:11,252
Your guitar still rests where
you left it. With just six

5
00:00:11,252 --> 00:00:14,090
strings, you wrote songs
that fought for your people.

6
00:00:16,250 --> 00:00:19,512
You weren't only a singer.
You taught children Qubee,

7
00:00:19,512 --> 00:00:22,210
so they could finally
write their own names.

8
00:00:23,250 --> 00:00:26,300
People listened to your
cassettes in secret. I kept

9
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:28,740
your old tape and
notebook of poems safe.

10
00:00:31,250 --> 00:00:34,446
You wrote these words in
blue ink. Before leaving for

11
00:00:34,446 --> 00:00:36,950
Finfinnee, you sang to me:
'Mother of a hero, do not

12
00:00:36,950 --> 00:00:37,950
weep.'

13
00:00:39,250 --> 00:00:42,510
Then in August ninety-six,
a bullet in Finfinnee

14
00:00:42,510 --> 00:00:45,810
silenced you. Since that
night, this house has lived

15
00:00:45,810 --> 00:00:46,810
in silence.

16
00:00:48,250 --> 00:00:51,265
Tonight, I refuse the
silence. I am not a singer,

17
00:00:51,265 --> 00:00:53,720
my son—only a mother
who misses you. I will try

18
00:00:53,720 --> 00:00:54,720
one note.

19
00:00:56,250 --> 00:00:59,664
I pluck one worn
string. They killed the

20
00:00:59,664 --> 00:01:01,590
singer—never the song.

21
00:01:02,250 --> 00:01:04,732
Listen, my son. Beyond
my doorway, a new

22
00:01:04,732 --> 00:01:07,550
generation—not even born
back then—knows every

23
00:01:07,550 --> 00:01:08,550
word you wrote.

24
00:01:10,250 --> 00:01:13,306
Look—through the rain,
they are singing your voice

25
00:01:13,306 --> 00:01:16,550
back to you. And tonight,
my son... I am not weeping.
